 we give  um a serotonin reuptake inhibiting drug  will treat the symptoms of the  depression by normalizing serotonin  well  um researched by several groups  including the work done here at Yale by  my colleague Dennis Charney and Pedro  Delgado George henniger and others they  showed that if you deplete the body of  Serotonin you don't produce depression  if you deplete the body of  norepinephrine you don't produce  depression and that the biology of  depression is is is more complicated in  some ways this was a kind of  intellectual crisis moment for the  thinking about the biology of depression  and and the way that uh Dr Charney and I  reasoned our way around this problem was  to say well maybe if depression doesn't  predominantly live in the serotonin or  norepinephrine nerve cells themselves  maybe depression lives in the targets of  of norepinephrine and serotonin the  higher brain centers and the two main  brain centers we thought of were the  cerebral cortex the highest executive  control of our thought and behavior and  emotion and the limbic system the  generators are drivers of emotional  experience in the brain  and and with that came a kind of an aha  moment which is if  if  um depression is a cortical disorder  not not a brain stem disorder then the  intrinsic signaling mechanisms of these  higher brain centers the main  information highways of the higher brain  centers  should be involved in depression in its  treatment  and those information higher uh  information highways involve the  neurotransmitter glutamate as the main  excitatory neurotransmitter of the  cortex like 90 percent of the synapses  so we thought Let's test a drug that  blocks one of these glutamate receptors  the drug we chose to do that was  ketamine and what we found was this  rapid Improvement in mood Within  24 hours say of of a single dose Academy  in a large number of the people we  tested what is the dosage idea when it  comes to ketamine is it the type of  thing that is a 